00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:51.800
DAVID KROODSMA: So
Global Fishing Watch.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:55.410
So who here has gone fishing?

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:56.610
Most of you.

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:59.490
Who here has been on an
industrial fishing boat

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:02.410
at some point in their life?

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:02.974
Where was it?

00:03:02.974 --> 00:03:03.640
And what was it?

00:03:06.496 --> 00:03:07.419
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:07.419 --> 00:03:08.210
DAVID KROODSMA: OK.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.910
So I probably won't be
able to find that boat.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:19.845
But most of us when we think
of fishing, think of this.

00:03:19.845 --> 00:03:21.220
And this is what
I do as fishing.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:22.990
And this is what most people do.

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:25.120
But most of the world's
fish are actually

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:27.060
caught by boats like this.

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.180
This is a picture of China.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.940
Each of these boats is
about 100-feet long.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:40.220
And the way that China
manages its fishery in order

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:45.420
to prevent overfishing is they
have a three-month moratorium

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:45.920
on fishing.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:47.840
At the end of which, everyone
is allowed to go out.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:49.506
And this is at the
end of the moratorium

00:03:49.506 --> 00:03:52.100
when all the fishing vessels are
about to go out to the ocean.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:53.516
And so that gives
you a sense when

00:03:53.516 --> 00:03:57.470
you can see all the boats at
once, just what is heading out

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:58.580
for you if you're a fish.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:04.390
And this is really
remarkable, but this is not

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:05.590
to pick on China.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:08.020
This is happening
all over the world.

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:12.790
And you know, it goes over out
into the ocean and extracts

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:14.620
fish from this shared
global resource.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.010
And it's kind of an ultimate
tragedy of the commons.

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:20.950
And the reason we
care about this

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:25.110
is because fishing is
such a huge problem.

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:28.380
So about 1 billion
people in the world

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:31.410
rely on fish as their
primary source of protein,

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:33.570
but the oceans are
very overfished.

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:36.610
90% of apex species have
disappeared in our lifetime.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.480
So that's big things
like shark and tuna.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:42.360
About 85% of the
world's fisheries

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:45.180
are either overfished,
at capacity,

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:48.559
or recovering from having
already been overfished.

00:04:48.559 --> 00:04:50.100
And perhaps the most
important number

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:54.210
here is this one, 1 in 5, which
is the amount of fish which,

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:58.807
as we call IUU, or Illegal,
Unregulated, or Undocumented.

00:04:58.807 --> 00:05:01.390
Potentially, it's fish where we
don't know where it came from.

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:03.510
And if you imagine you have
a huge shared resource,

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:05.520
and you're not even able
to measure where 20% of it

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.603
comes from, that's one of
the reasons you're going

00:05:07.603 --> 00:05:09.900
to have an overfishing problem.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.030
At the same time, this
is a problem which really

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.410
requires a global approach.

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:17.100
38% of fish is traded
internationally.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:20.050
55% comes from developing
countries with an export

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:22.210
value of $60 billion.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:24.490
So in summary, overfishing
is a huge problem.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:26.560
It desperately
needs transparency

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.720
and requires a global
approach to address,

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:32.440
which is why we developed
Global Fishing Watch.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:36.880
So Global Fishing
Watch takes data

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.650
from transceivers on boats which
broadcasts their locations.

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:42.370
And these are big, industrial
boats, not the small ones.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:44.830
And maps out how
they're moving, and then

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:47.410
identifies where and
when fishing is talking.

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:49.480
If you go to
globalfishingwatch.org,

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:50.980
you can look at our map.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:52.720
It's almost near real-time.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:56.560
So it shows fishing from
3 days ago to 5 years ago.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:57.540
And it looks like this.

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:08.065
It animates and shows you
where fishing is happening.

00:06:08.065 --> 00:06:10.440
I love looking at this because
every time you look at it,

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:11.440
I see something new.

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:14.809
You can see patterns of
fishing along the equator,

00:06:14.809 --> 00:06:16.600
where there's upwelling
bringing nutrients.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:18.460
A lot of tuna fishing
happens there.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:21.345
You can see fishing all
along the continental shelves

00:06:21.345 --> 00:06:22.970
because that's where
a lot of nutrients

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:24.220
are, a lot of fishing is.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:27.400
You can see political
boundaries where

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:29.650
a lot of some type
of fishing vessels

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:31.870
aren't allowed to fish within
certain country waters,

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:33.760
so they fish right
along the edge of it.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:35.320
And you can also
see where people

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:38.285
don't respect the boundaries
of different countries,

00:06:38.285 --> 00:06:39.910
like you can see
Australia right there.

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:42.550
You can see fishing happening
right along the edge of it.

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:47.660
You can see in Africa people
ignoring some of those lines.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:52.930
So I'm going to now
switch to our website

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:56.380
so you can see
what it actually--

00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:58.310
So you can click on
individual vessels.

00:06:58.310 --> 00:07:00.370
So this is actually
our live website.

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:01.780
And you can watch them move.

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:05.990
So here, I clicked on this
one, which is the Chi Yang I.

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:09.175
And I should explain, each
of these little yellow dots

00:07:09.175 --> 00:07:13.110
is where we think a
fishing activity happened.

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:14.630
And so if you hit
Play, you can now

00:07:14.630 --> 00:07:17.600
watch where this boat is moving.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:20.450
And this is a Malaysian
boat, the Chi Yang

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:24.320
I. It's a longliner, which means
it sets long lines of hooks.

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:30.800
Sometimes, as long as 100
kilometers, and then retrieves

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:31.460
them.

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:32.930
And so here, you can see
it moving back and forth

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:33.600
across the ocean.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:35.600
And all the other dots are where
there's other boats fishing

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:36.300
here as well.

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:45.790
You can see
interesting patterns.

00:07:45.790 --> 00:07:49.257
Such as here, here's an island
in the Southern Atlantic.

00:07:49.257 --> 00:07:51.340
You can see fishing happening
right along the edge

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:52.720
where the continental
shelf ends.

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:56.682
And you can see that there
is clearly some type of geo--

00:07:56.682 --> 00:07:59.140
some type of physical property
of the ocean where upwelling

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:00.348
happens right along the edge.

00:08:00.348 --> 00:08:02.660
And that's where fishermen
are directing their boats.

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:04.660
And so there's all types
of interesting patterns

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:06.280
we can use to gain
insight in how

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:08.710
to better manage our oceans.

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:09.820
So that's the tool.

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:11.424
That's the exciting
thing we've built.

00:08:11.424 --> 00:08:13.840
And we're going to spend the
rest of the time here talking

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:14.819
about how we built it.

00:08:14.819 --> 00:08:16.360
And hopefully, then
empower you to be

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:23.100
able to do some of the same
types of machine learning

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:24.121
techniques.

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:32.030
So the core
technology to this is

00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:35.720
called AIS, which stands for
the creatively named Automatic

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:37.760
Identification System,
which is something

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:40.880
that all large fishing
vessels are required to carry.

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:42.080
And it's a safety mechanism.

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:44.538
The idea is you broadcast your
location so that other boats

00:08:44.538 --> 00:08:46.020
nearby can see where you are.

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:48.103
This is very important in
some parts of the ocean.

00:08:48.103 --> 00:08:50.552
Here's a chart of boats
in the English Channel.

00:08:50.552 --> 00:08:52.010
And all of these
boats want to make

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:53.150
sure they see where
the other ones are,

00:08:53.150 --> 00:08:54.483
so they don't collide with them.

00:08:54.483 --> 00:08:57.990
If you're in a boat with an
AIS device, it looks like this.

00:08:57.990 --> 00:09:00.572
And you can see
it just brought--

00:09:00.572 --> 00:09:02.780
you can see where all the
other boats are nearby you.

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:05.630
It tells you the name of that
boat, which direction it's

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:08.840
headed, its speed, all
types of useful information

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:11.510
to help you not collide with it.

00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:14.090
These signals though, they
can be picked up by satellite.

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:17.757
And also, by receivers along
the land, terrestrial antennas.

00:09:17.757 --> 00:09:19.340
In the last few
years, a few companies

00:09:19.340 --> 00:09:21.620
have launched satellites
which collect this data,

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:24.480
and we have finally been able to
build a global database of AIS

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:27.130
signals.

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:30.342
And that is what we have been
collecting at Global Fishing

00:09:30.342 --> 00:09:31.300
Watch and working with.

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:39.020
The AIS message looks like this.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:41.330
It's an NMEA
message is the type.

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:44.990
There's a Googler who wrote a
library that we use to parse it

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:46.219
into a readable JSON file.

00:09:46.219 --> 00:09:47.510
And this is what it looks like.

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:50.540
It has information about
the latitude, the longitude,

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:53.150
the course, the
speed, the heading,

00:09:53.150 --> 00:09:56.680
as well as some
other information.

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:59.749
And we get about 20 million
of these data points a day.

00:09:59.749 --> 00:10:01.540
As you can see, like
all other data graphs,

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:04.660
it's going up because
there's more now.

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:06.700
We have about half
a billion a month.

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:09.520
In 2012, you were getting far
fewer because fewer boats had

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:10.780
this technology.

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:13.780
And we had fewer satellites
and fewer terrestrial antennas.

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:16.360
So we're getting more and
more data every month.

00:10:19.985 --> 00:10:22.360
And when you map out what all
the boats in the world look

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:23.120
like, it looks like this.

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:25.600
There's somewhere between
300,000 and 400,000 vessels

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:26.764
were AIS.

00:10:26.764 --> 00:10:28.680
This is all of them, not
just fishing vessels.

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:31.580
And about 80,000 of these
are fishing vessels.

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:35.470
And that's what we identify
to determine fishing effort.

00:10:41.637 --> 00:10:42.220
AMY UNRUH: OK.

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:43.992
So this is a lot of data.

00:10:43.992 --> 00:10:45.450
When I started
looking at it, I was

00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:51.870
startled to find that every day,
15 gigs was getting dumped--

00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:56.610
of this data was getting dumped
into Google Cloud Storage.

00:10:56.610 --> 00:11:00.090
And so not only is it a lot of
data to kind of shuffle around.

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:02.650
But if you're going to analyze
it in any meaningful way,

00:11:02.650 --> 00:11:05.310
it requires a lot
of compute power.

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:08.940
And so Global Fishing
Watch would kind of

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:10.980
like to not have to
worry about that,

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:13.200
about shuffling large
amounts of data around,

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:15.820
about provisioning
VMs when they need it.

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:18.660
And so you can probably
guess the punchline--

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:21.590
Google Cloud platform
to the rescue.

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:26.520
So what I'm going to talk
about is the workflow

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:29.070
that they're using, and how
they put together some Cloud

00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:32.640
platform products
to do what David

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:36.460
described on the
globalfishingwatch.org site.

00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:43.000
And I'm going to focus for this
talk on Dataflow and Cloud ML

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:43.530
Engine.

00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:47.310
But they're also making a
lot of use of other stuff.

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:49.984
In particular, BigQuery
and Cloud Storage.

00:11:49.984 --> 00:11:52.400
David's going to talk a little
bit more about the BigQuery

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:53.955
later.

00:11:53.955 --> 00:11:54.780
OK.

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:57.720
So this is kind of
a typical workflow.

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:00.690
And they actually
have several of them

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:02.740
that follow this pattern.

00:12:02.740 --> 00:12:06.220
So first of all, a lot
of ship movement data.

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:08.610
Remember, it's like
about 15 gigs of day,

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:12.390
is getting dumped
into Cloud Storage.

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:16.980
And then, periodically
in batch mode,

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:21.570
they're running Dataflow
jobs to process this data,

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:25.480
to transform it,
to aggregate it.

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:36.000
And the output of that Dataflow
batch pipeline is features.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:39.510
Using the term in
the sense of input

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:41.450
to a machine learning model.

00:12:41.450 --> 00:12:48.530
So the outputting data that
is cleansed and aggregated

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:50.720
and transformed into
something that they

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:53.510
want to feed into
a TensorFlow model.

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:57.780
So then you can probably guess
the next part of this workflow

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:01.945
is TensorFlow and
Cloud ML Engine.

00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:07.450
The features are
being generated.

00:13:07.450 --> 00:13:09.820
And then, there are
sort of three parts

00:13:09.820 --> 00:13:11.120
to what happens next.

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:16.780
So the first part is generating
the training data, of course.

00:13:16.780 --> 00:13:20.290
And as those of you who
have done this kind of thing

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:23.510
might know, that can definitely
be non-trivial at times.

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:25.510
David is going to talk a
little more about that.

00:13:25.510 --> 00:13:30.280
And then, they've built
some very interesting

00:13:30.280 --> 00:13:33.040
convolutional neural nets
to do classification.

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:35.060
You'll hear a little
bit more about those.

00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:39.430
So label the training
data, train the model.

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:42.190
So they're using
Cloud ML Engine to do

00:13:42.190 --> 00:13:46.010
distributed training of the
model, of the TensorFlow model.

00:13:46.010 --> 00:13:48.290
And then once the
model is trained,

00:13:48.290 --> 00:13:51.140
they use it for inference.

00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:53.980
So to dive a little
bit more into some

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:55.820
of the pieces of this.

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:58.900
So probably some of
you who have been

00:13:58.900 --> 00:14:01.150
to talks that go into
this in more detail,

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:03.400
but for those of
you who haven't, let

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:07.780
me say a little bit first about
Apache Beam and Cloud Dataflow.

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:14.020
So Apache Beam is a unified
model for building data

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:16.900
processing pipelines
that can handle

00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:22.150
both bounded and unbounded data,
batch and stream processing.

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:26.620
And Apache Beam is
also a set of SDKs

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:29.790
for building these pipelines.

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:33.300
And one particularly
salient point

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:35.400
about the model for the
purposes of this talk

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:39.040
is the way that Beam does
event time processing.

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:44.480
So you can tell Beam, I want
you to analyze this data

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:47.920
with respect to the time
it arrived at the pipeline.

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:51.160
Or you can tell it, I want to
analyze this data with respect

00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:54.130
to when it was
actually generated.

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:57.020
And we need to do that latter
thing for the Global Fishing

00:14:57.020 --> 00:14:57.520
Watch data.

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:00.970
We want to say, do your
analysis with respect

00:15:00.970 --> 00:15:05.600
to the time this was
actually logged by the ship.

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:09.260
So then, Cloud Dataflow
is a managed service

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:12.240
for executing these
Beam pipelines.

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:17.150
And one nice thing about it is
that it supports auto-scaling.

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:20.890
So it will provision
workers up and down

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:24.020
as it needs them while
it's running a big job.

00:15:27.010 --> 00:15:28.160
OK.

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:32.490
So that was Dataflow, the
middle green piece there.

00:15:32.490 --> 00:15:36.780
Let me talk a little bit about
TensorFlow and Cloud ML Engine.

00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:40.890
So again, some of you have
probably heard about TensorFlow

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:42.550
in the past couple of days.

00:15:42.550 --> 00:15:48.580
This is an open source
platform for doing

00:15:48.580 --> 00:15:53.080
numerical computations
with data flow graphs.

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:55.240
But in particular,
it is particularly

00:15:55.240 --> 00:16:00.660
good for building and training
deep neural net models.

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:03.430
It has a distributed runtime.

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:05.940
It supports
auto-differentiation.

00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:09.550
It was sort of built
for deep learning.

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:15.890
So Cloud ML Engine,
which those of you

00:16:15.890 --> 00:16:20.200
who attended yesterday's keynote
know has just gone to GA then,

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:24.280
is a managed service
for training,

00:16:24.280 --> 00:16:28.330
doing distributed training
of your TensorFlow models.

00:16:28.330 --> 00:16:31.660
With GPUs are now
available, if you like.

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:35.590
And then, for serving the
model once you've trained it.

00:16:35.590 --> 00:16:37.740
So we'll talk a little
bit more about that later.

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:39.115
DAVID KROODSMA:
And I should note

00:16:39.115 --> 00:16:41.950
that we've been using these
GPUs and see about a 10 times

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:44.770
improvement in our model
training and inference.

00:16:44.770 --> 00:16:47.235
Using the GPUs over the CPUs
has been incredibly useful.

00:16:47.235 --> 00:16:49.360
Especially right now, I'm
actually having some back

00:16:49.360 --> 00:16:51.193
and forth with our
machine learning engineer

00:16:51.193 --> 00:16:52.960
and doing lots of
iterations on the model,

00:16:52.960 --> 00:16:56.146
and it's really helpful to
have those fast processors.

00:16:59.777 --> 00:17:00.360
AMY UNRUH: OK.

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:06.280
So that was a workflow that
Global Fishing Watch is using.

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:08.329
But actually, it's much
more general than that.

00:17:08.329 --> 00:17:12.130
And this is kind of a
useful and common design

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:15.579
pattern where you want to
do-- you've got a lot of data.

00:17:15.579 --> 00:17:18.460
You want to do a lot of
pre-processing and feature

00:17:18.460 --> 00:17:22.660
generation, and then you
want to train a model.

00:17:22.660 --> 00:17:28.480
And so if you do this
all in the cloud,

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:30.234
you don't have to
worry about scaling up.

00:17:30.234 --> 00:17:32.650
You don't have to worry about
shuffling very large amounts

00:17:32.650 --> 00:17:33.700
of data around.

00:17:33.700 --> 00:17:35.400
And that's kind of
what this suggests.

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:37.900
Do your pre-processing
with Dataflow,

00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:41.965
send the result to Cloud ML
to do distributed training

00:17:41.965 --> 00:17:43.590
of your TensorFlow model.

00:17:48.514 --> 00:17:49.930
DAVID KROODSMA: I
want to talk now

00:17:49.930 --> 00:17:51.388
about how we actually
built the net

00:17:51.388 --> 00:17:54.360
and how we got the
training data for it.

00:17:54.360 --> 00:17:56.320
So we have two main
classification problems

00:17:56.320 --> 00:17:57.670
with Global Fishing Watch.

00:17:57.670 --> 00:18:00.746
One is vessel identity.

00:18:00.746 --> 00:18:03.370
So identifying what type of boat
it is and what type of fishing

00:18:03.370 --> 00:18:04.420
it is doing.

00:18:04.420 --> 00:18:06.010
It matters a lot
for conservation

00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:08.590
and for marine spatial
planning whether or not

00:18:08.590 --> 00:18:10.390
the boat is fishing
with a trawling

00:18:10.390 --> 00:18:12.250
net, which drags
along the bottom,

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:15.640
or if it's fishing with
these long lines of hooks.

00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:18.520
And they don't tell you what
it is in the AIS message,

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:21.430
so you have to look at how
the boat moves to identify it.

00:18:21.430 --> 00:18:22.900
The second problem
is identifying

00:18:22.900 --> 00:18:24.524
when they are actually
fishing and when

00:18:24.524 --> 00:18:27.059
they're just transiting
from one place to the next.

00:18:27.059 --> 00:18:29.350
So here, you can see these
are three of the major types

00:18:29.350 --> 00:18:30.550
of fishing boats.

00:18:30.550 --> 00:18:33.100
There is a trawler,
longliner, and purse seiner.

00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:34.390
They move differently.

00:18:34.390 --> 00:18:36.670
There is some pictures
of these large vessels.

00:18:36.670 --> 00:18:38.150
And your eye can
see the difference

00:18:38.150 --> 00:18:41.290
and so we can train a machine
to see a difference as well.

00:18:41.290 --> 00:18:42.640
The hard part?

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:44.260
In order to train
this model, we had

00:18:44.260 --> 00:18:46.750
to get a few thousand
examples of these boats.

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:48.880
And we did that by
matching the AIS data

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:51.430
to official registries,
which involved a lot of fuzzy

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:52.895
logic and fuzzy matching.

00:18:52.895 --> 00:18:54.520
From that, we got a
few thousand boats,

00:18:54.520 --> 00:18:59.521
from which we were
able to identify

00:18:59.521 --> 00:19:00.770
a few hundred thousands boats.

00:19:00.770 --> 00:19:01.840
And I should add
that we were also

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:03.220
identifying non-fishing boats.

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:06.294
Because remember, all the
boats with AIS, most of them

00:19:06.294 --> 00:19:07.210
are not fishing boats.

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:08.710
So we have to be
able to tell what's

00:19:08.710 --> 00:19:13.060
a tugboat, what's a longline
fishing boat, what's a tanker.

00:19:13.060 --> 00:19:16.810
Second problem is identifying
when the boat is fishing.

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:18.310
And to do that,
ideally you would

00:19:18.310 --> 00:19:23.944
have some logbook
from the captain that

00:19:23.944 --> 00:19:25.360
would tell you
when and where they

00:19:25.360 --> 00:19:27.025
put their gear in the water.

00:19:27.025 --> 00:19:29.170
The problem is that,
1, captains rarely want

00:19:29.170 --> 00:19:30.810
to give you that information.

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:33.550
And 2, when you get it,
usually it's not exactly right.

00:19:33.550 --> 00:19:38.830
For instance, the latitude and
longitude may be slightly off

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:41.530
or the time stamp may be
in the wrong time zone.

00:19:41.530 --> 00:19:43.900
And so it's much
easier and sometimes

00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:46.570
more reliable to just look
at the tracks and hand label

00:19:46.570 --> 00:19:48.490
where you think
they are fishing.

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:50.350
And so this is what we did.

00:19:50.350 --> 00:19:53.590
And this was very, very tedious.

00:19:53.590 --> 00:19:58.210
I built a small
JavaScript interface

00:19:58.210 --> 00:20:02.140
through PYBOSSA, an open source
platform for crowdsourcing.

00:20:02.140 --> 00:20:05.590
And then, basically messaged
everyone on Slack every day

00:20:05.590 --> 00:20:09.490
to tell them they
had to do more tasks.

00:20:09.490 --> 00:20:12.010
And we made a little
competition out of it.

00:20:12.010 --> 00:20:14.710
Don't let Christian
crush us this week.

00:20:14.710 --> 00:20:17.800
And this went on for
about six weeks every day,

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:20.200
until we had labeled a few
hundred thousand points.

00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:26.210
And this is one
of the main things

00:20:26.210 --> 00:20:28.520
about this machine
learning techniques

00:20:28.520 --> 00:20:29.810
I've learned is that--

00:20:29.810 --> 00:20:32.722
how one of the major efforts
is getting the training data

00:20:32.722 --> 00:20:34.680
and getting it in the
right position before you

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:37.780
can even do any of the
fun model building.

00:20:40.930 --> 00:20:42.460
Our first algorithm
on fishing was

00:20:42.460 --> 00:20:48.462
a simple logistic regression
trained on the average speed,

00:20:48.462 --> 00:20:50.920
the standard deviation of speed,
and the standard deviation

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:52.600
of course as well
as those features

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:56.140
squared across a few
different time windows.

00:20:56.140 --> 00:20:59.650
The whole idea is, if a boat
is moving slowly, wiggling,

00:20:59.650 --> 00:21:02.220
and changing direction,
it's more likely fishing.

00:21:02.220 --> 00:21:04.660
And that model did
relatively well in accuracy.

00:21:04.660 --> 00:21:08.530
Overall accuracy, I
think just under 0.8.

00:21:08.530 --> 00:21:11.320
But then we've decided
we wanted to do--

00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:14.050
both for identifying
specific vessels and

00:21:14.050 --> 00:21:15.550
for identifying
this fishing effort,

00:21:15.550 --> 00:21:17.470
we wanted to build
a neural network

00:21:17.470 --> 00:21:20.480
to do this because we thought
we could do a little bit better.

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:22.360
And the problem with
using a neural network

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:24.310
is just the sheer
number of points.

00:21:24.310 --> 00:21:27.440
So you can think about it.

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:29.900
We want to look at the
whole path of a boat.

00:21:29.900 --> 00:21:32.944
And so you take maybe 10,000
data points of where it's been.

00:21:32.944 --> 00:21:34.360
And for each of
those data points,

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:39.640
you have like 10
different features,

00:21:39.640 --> 00:21:41.760
such as the speed,
such as the course,

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:45.735
such as the distance from
shore, the time of day.

00:21:45.735 --> 00:21:47.414
And you want to use
all that information

00:21:47.414 --> 00:21:49.330
to tell whether or not
what type of boat it is

00:21:49.330 --> 00:21:50.371
and whether it's fishing.

00:21:53.830 --> 00:21:57.530
So that's 10 features per
point and 10,000 points.

00:21:57.530 --> 00:21:58.970
That's over 100,000 features.

00:21:58.970 --> 00:22:02.870
You can't easily run a
fully-connected neural network

00:22:02.870 --> 00:22:06.500
on that, so we built a
convolutional neural net that

00:22:06.500 --> 00:22:08.477
does a convolution in time.

00:22:08.477 --> 00:22:10.685
And what that does is we
have 9 of these convolutions

00:22:10.685 --> 00:22:13.720
and that takes more
than 100,000 features

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:18.450
and brings it down to
around 2,000 features.

00:22:18.450 --> 00:22:21.030
And then after that, we have
2 fully-connected layers.

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:23.280
And that brings us down
to a little over a dozen

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:25.570
different vessel classes.

00:22:25.570 --> 00:22:27.300
And then we have a
similar architecture

00:22:27.300 --> 00:22:30.080
which figures out the--

00:22:30.080 --> 00:22:32.280
looks at each point and
identifies whether or not

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:33.530
we think it is fishing.

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:40.890
This is the detailed
architecture

00:22:40.890 --> 00:22:43.540
of the identification model.

00:22:43.540 --> 00:22:47.040
We're also identifying the
length and the engine power

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:48.542
and the weight of these boats.

00:22:48.542 --> 00:22:50.250
Obviously, I'm not
going to go over this,

00:22:50.250 --> 00:22:51.810
but we're working on
a scientific paper

00:22:51.810 --> 00:22:52.960
to write up these findings.

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:54.459
And you'll be able
to read much more

00:22:54.459 --> 00:22:57.305
about that in the supplemental
materials of that paper.

00:23:00.700 --> 00:23:01.990
So how does it work?

00:23:01.990 --> 00:23:03.090
It works pretty well.

00:23:03.090 --> 00:23:06.630
Here's a confusion matrix of
how the classification does

00:23:06.630 --> 00:23:08.427
on our different classes.

00:23:08.427 --> 00:23:09.760
These are the different classes.

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:11.850
The top one is cargo or tanker.

00:23:11.850 --> 00:23:14.550
Reefer is a type of
cargo vessel for fishing.

00:23:14.550 --> 00:23:16.802
And so you can see it
actually confuses it

00:23:16.802 --> 00:23:19.260
with something that you would
expect it to, because they're

00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:20.900
very similar types of boats.

00:23:20.900 --> 00:23:23.280
But for fishing boats, which
are all down on the bottom,

00:23:23.280 --> 00:23:25.980
it rarely confuses them
with non-fishing boats.

00:23:25.980 --> 00:23:30.090
So we're pretty happy
with this overall.

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:33.340
And then, identifying
fishing activity.

00:23:33.340 --> 00:23:35.796
It's a little bit better
than the logistic model.

00:23:35.796 --> 00:23:37.170
And we're making
some adjustments

00:23:37.170 --> 00:23:38.419
to make even better than that.

00:23:38.419 --> 00:23:41.040
So it performs better than
the logistic, which is good.

00:23:43.980 --> 00:23:45.730
And we can, for the
first time, build maps

00:23:45.730 --> 00:23:49.140
like this, which is where
we think trawlers are

00:23:49.140 --> 00:23:50.390
fishing in the world's oceans.

00:23:50.390 --> 00:23:53.380
And trawlers usually
stay close to the coast

00:23:53.380 --> 00:23:56.259
because they drag their
nets along the ocean floor.

00:23:56.259 --> 00:23:58.300
So this is a map where we
think all trawlers were

00:23:58.300 --> 00:24:01.030
fishing in 2016.

00:24:01.030 --> 00:24:02.110
This is longliners.

00:24:02.110 --> 00:24:04.870
They set drifting long
lines out in the ocean,

00:24:04.870 --> 00:24:07.180
and so they cover
all the oceans.

00:24:07.180 --> 00:24:09.790
And then, purse seiners,
which set these nets

00:24:09.790 --> 00:24:10.960
around schools of fish.

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:11.530
Often, tuna.

00:24:11.530 --> 00:24:13.570
And you can see them
fishing along the equator

00:24:13.570 --> 00:24:17.950
as well as off the
coasts of some countries.

00:24:21.404 --> 00:24:22.570
Again, why does this matter?

00:24:22.570 --> 00:24:25.560
Why do we care about identifying
where and when fishing happens?

00:24:25.560 --> 00:24:27.920
Well, here is-- I shouldn't
have showed that yet.

00:24:27.920 --> 00:24:29.680
Anyway.

00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:36.180
So this is a map of the Central
Pacific in Global Fishing

00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:36.850
Watch.

00:24:36.850 --> 00:24:39.830
And I'm going to zoom into
the country, Kiribati.

00:24:39.830 --> 00:24:45.943
Halfway between-- oops.

00:24:45.943 --> 00:24:51.520
So the country Kiribati has the
world's largest marine UNESCO

00:24:51.520 --> 00:24:52.480
World Heritage site.

00:24:54.845 --> 00:24:55.970
Can you see it on this map?

00:25:01.850 --> 00:25:05.250
It is right there.

00:25:05.250 --> 00:25:07.580
So even though it was a
World Heritage-- and this

00:25:07.580 --> 00:25:08.960
is from 2014.

00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:10.770
Even though it as a
World Heritage site,

00:25:10.770 --> 00:25:12.620
industrial fishing
was still allowed.

00:25:12.620 --> 00:25:15.760
So you see lots of
these yellow dots in it.

00:25:15.760 --> 00:25:22.070
However, on January 1, 2015,
the President of Kiribati

00:25:22.070 --> 00:25:24.530
decided to make fishing,
industrial fishing,

00:25:24.530 --> 00:25:28.640
illegal in this area, the
Phoenix Islands Protected Area,

00:25:28.640 --> 00:25:29.960
or PIPA.

00:25:29.960 --> 00:25:32.210
And so all boats were
going to have to clear out.

00:25:32.210 --> 00:25:33.584
And so the question
was, could we

00:25:33.584 --> 00:25:36.100
use Global Fishing Watch
to identify whether or not

00:25:36.100 --> 00:25:39.740
this new boundary
was being obeyed?

00:25:39.740 --> 00:25:44.070
And I should emphasize, this
is the size of California,

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:45.230
that red box.

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:47.480
Care is one of the
world's poorest countries.

00:25:47.480 --> 00:25:50.200
They have like one patrol
boat for that entire thing.

00:25:50.200 --> 00:25:54.482
So imagine you have to patrol
California with one boat.

00:25:54.482 --> 00:25:56.940
And so what we did is you kind
of wait to see what happens.

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:02.160
And now it's playing forward.

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:05.670
This is a 2-month
period of time.

00:26:05.670 --> 00:26:09.780
And now we're into 2015, a
little bit of 2014 still.

00:26:09.780 --> 00:26:16.140
And as we get into all
of 2015, they clear out.

00:26:16.140 --> 00:26:18.150
And we know the
boats didn't just

00:26:18.150 --> 00:26:19.860
turn off their AIS
because we were

00:26:19.860 --> 00:26:22.020
able to track what
happened to all those boats

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:24.450
that left and saw that they
actually went somewhere else

00:26:24.450 --> 00:26:25.590
and fished.

00:26:25.590 --> 00:26:27.720
And so it's really
exciting that we're

00:26:27.720 --> 00:26:33.210
able to identify this park.

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:40.920
Now, a few months after it
closed, a boat, the Marshalls

00:26:40.920 --> 00:26:45.150
203, we saw fishing just right
inside the line right there.

00:26:45.150 --> 00:26:48.690
And Kiribati authorities were
notified, and they went--

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:52.560
and their one boat escorted that
fishing vessel back to port.

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:56.820
And they went to the
captain and said,

00:26:56.820 --> 00:26:58.860
you are fishing illegally.

00:26:58.860 --> 00:27:00.570
We want to fine
you for doing this.

00:27:00.570 --> 00:27:03.234
And the captain said, you
can't prove I was fishing.

00:27:03.234 --> 00:27:03.900
How do you know?

00:27:03.900 --> 00:27:05.964
I'll see you in court.

00:27:05.964 --> 00:27:08.380
And so they actually called
up SkyTruth and Global Fishing

00:27:08.380 --> 00:27:10.460
Watch and said, hey, can
you guys look at this boat

00:27:10.460 --> 00:27:12.250
and see how it is moving,
tell us whether it was fishing

00:27:12.250 --> 00:27:12.940
or not?

00:27:12.940 --> 00:27:14.620
And we sent them
basically something

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:17.470
like this with an analysis
on it as well as the results

00:27:17.470 --> 00:27:18.940
of our fishing algorithm.

00:27:18.940 --> 00:27:20.800
They showed that to
the captain of the boat

00:27:20.800 --> 00:27:22.890
and he said, OK, I'll settle.

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:32.520
And it resulted in a $2
million fine, which is about 1%

00:27:32.520 --> 00:27:35.580
of the country's GDP.

00:27:35.580 --> 00:27:39.480
And so it was extremely--

00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:42.945
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:48.370 --> 00:27:51.260
I also enjoy a lot of
data science work on this.

00:27:51.260 --> 00:27:55.090
So once we have this big
data set, there's a lot of--

00:27:55.090 --> 00:27:57.490
we want to be able to look in
all different types of way.

00:27:57.490 --> 00:27:59.080
But it's still
several terabytes.

00:27:59.080 --> 00:28:02.290
And so we load it into BigQuery,
and then use various tools

00:28:02.290 --> 00:28:04.962
to query and BigQuery to
slice in different ways.

00:28:04.962 --> 00:28:05.920
And this is an example.

00:28:05.920 --> 00:28:07.370
I do a lot of work like this.

00:28:07.370 --> 00:28:08.750
This is an IPython notebook.

00:28:08.750 --> 00:28:10.780
I run the write I wrote
to write a query to make

00:28:10.780 --> 00:28:14.980
this graphic, which it's
not impressionist art.

00:28:14.980 --> 00:28:19.460
Someone saw this and
said, congratulations.

00:28:19.460 --> 00:28:22.080
But what this is, is the
x-axis is the day of the year.

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:23.769
The y-axis is degrees latitude.

00:28:23.769 --> 00:28:26.060
And so you can see when and
where fishing is happening.

00:28:26.060 --> 00:28:27.620
You can see we have a lot
more fishing in the Northern

00:28:27.620 --> 00:28:28.220
Hemisphere.

00:28:28.220 --> 00:28:29.960
That's partially
because our data set.

00:28:29.960 --> 00:28:31.910
You can see people
take the weekends off.

00:28:31.910 --> 00:28:34.850
That's what each of
those stripes are.

00:28:34.850 --> 00:28:36.920
You can see that
three-month closure period I

00:28:36.920 --> 00:28:39.730
was telling you about in China.

00:28:39.730 --> 00:28:41.750
You can also see
that China actually--

00:28:41.750 --> 00:28:44.000
people in China don't take
the weekends off as much as

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:47.502
in North America and Europe.

00:28:47.502 --> 00:28:48.710
And you can see other things.

00:28:48.710 --> 00:28:51.410
You can see that in Antarctica,
there's very little fishing.

00:28:51.410 --> 00:28:55.456
And it happens only in
the Antarctic summer.

00:28:55.456 --> 00:28:56.830
And what's exciting
about this is

00:28:56.830 --> 00:28:58.300
every time I look
at this data, I

00:28:58.300 --> 00:29:00.160
make kind of a new
discovery, something

00:29:00.160 --> 00:29:01.660
no one's ever seen before.

00:29:01.660 --> 00:29:04.346
And that's only possible because
we have these big data tools,

00:29:04.346 --> 00:29:06.220
like BigQuery, that
allow us to quickly slice

00:29:06.220 --> 00:29:08.230
this data in different ways.

00:29:08.230 --> 00:29:10.360
We also are starting to
use Earth Engine, which

00:29:10.360 --> 00:29:14.710
is a Google platform that
allows you to do calculations

00:29:14.710 --> 00:29:15.775
on very large rasters.

00:29:18.520 --> 00:29:21.760
So we can actually make our
data, turn it into a raster,

00:29:21.760 --> 00:29:23.500
and load it into
Earth Engine, and then

00:29:23.500 --> 00:29:31.180
look at how it compares
to various climatological

00:29:31.180 --> 00:29:34.510
or other types of
global data sets.

00:29:34.510 --> 00:29:37.150
And this just shows where all
the vessels from Spain fish.

00:29:37.150 --> 00:29:38.608
And actually, it's
quite remarkable

00:29:38.608 --> 00:29:41.584
if you look at how
much Spain is fishing

00:29:41.584 --> 00:29:42.750
all over the world's oceans.

00:29:54.007 --> 00:29:54.590
AMY UNRUH: OK.

00:29:54.590 --> 00:29:57.480
So this is where I
came into the story.

00:29:57.480 --> 00:30:00.240
So I wanted to see--

00:30:00.240 --> 00:30:02.610
this is obviously
very, very cool stuff.

00:30:02.610 --> 00:30:04.020
So I wanted to
see, what would it

00:30:04.020 --> 00:30:09.600
take to basically
build on top of this,

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:15.430
to see if we could generate
close to real-time predictions

00:30:15.430 --> 00:30:20.100
of fishing activity as
all this data gets dumped

00:30:20.100 --> 00:30:23.820
into cloud storage every day?

00:30:23.820 --> 00:30:27.480
So it turns out that
the answer is yes.

00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:30.930
And it was pretty
straightforward.

00:30:30.930 --> 00:30:33.450
I was able to do what
I'm going to talk about

00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:35.490
in just a few weeks.

00:30:35.490 --> 00:30:39.210
And very little
needed to be changed

00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:44.140
in terms of how I hooked into
what they had already built.

00:30:44.140 --> 00:30:49.320
And so primary
reason for that is

00:30:49.320 --> 00:30:53.760
that it's very straightforward
to take the Dataflow pipeline

00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:57.990
that we talked about earlier,
that Global Fishing Watch has

00:30:57.990 --> 00:31:02.850
been running in batch, using
to generate feature data.

00:31:02.850 --> 00:31:06.614
Because Beam has this unified
streaming batch model,

00:31:06.614 --> 00:31:08.280
it was very straightforward
to turn this

00:31:08.280 --> 00:31:10.680
into a streaming pipeline.

00:31:10.680 --> 00:31:12.750
And so essentially,
all I had to do

00:31:12.750 --> 00:31:17.430
was to change the so-called
sources and syncs, the inputs

00:31:17.430 --> 00:31:20.010
and outputs of the pipeline.

00:31:20.010 --> 00:31:25.800
So I did some work
to stream data

00:31:25.800 --> 00:31:29.580
into the pipeline using Pub/Sub.

00:31:29.580 --> 00:31:37.710
And then, the pipeline was
also modified to emit data

00:31:37.710 --> 00:31:40.300
to another Pub/Sub topic.

00:31:40.300 --> 00:31:42.840
So streaming in, streaming out.

00:31:42.840 --> 00:31:46.210
But the logic of the
pipeline could stay the same.

00:31:46.210 --> 00:31:47.640
And so that was really nice.

00:31:47.640 --> 00:31:49.990
It was really straightforward.

00:31:49.990 --> 00:31:54.490
And then I added a
new pipeline that

00:31:54.490 --> 00:32:01.600
consumed that output stream and
did some further derivations

00:32:01.600 --> 00:32:04.390
using the derived features.

00:32:04.390 --> 00:32:11.350
And using Cloud ML Engine and
the trained TensorFlow model

00:32:11.350 --> 00:32:16.000
to make predictions about where
fishing activity was happening.

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:19.660
So this was the second
of the two models

00:32:19.660 --> 00:32:21.505
that David was talking
about, the model

00:32:21.505 --> 00:32:25.100
that predicts fishing activity.

00:32:25.100 --> 00:32:27.214
OK.

00:32:27.214 --> 00:32:29.510
So what this second
pipeline does

00:32:29.510 --> 00:32:33.440
is basically surface
in near real-time,

00:32:33.440 --> 00:32:37.850
where are the fishing activity
hotspots at a given time?

00:32:37.850 --> 00:32:40.400
So let me show you what
kind of the result of that,

00:32:40.400 --> 00:32:42.900
and then I'll dig into the
tech that made it work.

00:32:51.210 --> 00:32:57.580
So this is being displayed
on CARTO, a cool site.

00:32:57.580 --> 00:33:03.160
This is real data from
a couple of weeks ago.

00:33:03.160 --> 00:33:07.120
And when I hit Play,
what it's going to show

00:33:07.120 --> 00:33:14.380
is basically, over the course
of about 10 days, what fishing

00:33:14.380 --> 00:33:16.750
activity hotspots did we find?

00:33:16.750 --> 00:33:22.120
So the heat maps are
just showing magnitude

00:33:22.120 --> 00:33:23.140
of fishing in the area.

00:33:23.140 --> 00:33:26.080
It might have been
more than one boat.

00:33:26.080 --> 00:33:31.150
And over time, you can
see those shift around.

00:33:31.150 --> 00:33:36.620
It's even a little more dramatic
if I zoom out a bit here.

00:33:36.620 --> 00:33:37.580
OK.

00:33:37.580 --> 00:33:43.220
So you can think of this
as being in near real time.

00:33:43.220 --> 00:33:46.580
As the data comes in,
we're saying, all right.

00:33:46.580 --> 00:33:47.930
There's fishing activity here.

00:33:47.930 --> 00:33:51.220
There's fishing activity here
at a given point in time.

00:33:51.220 --> 00:33:54.230
And then, we kind of have
a sliding time window.

00:33:54.230 --> 00:33:58.520
And so that can help Global
Fishing Watch give them

00:33:58.520 --> 00:33:59.660
indication--

00:33:59.660 --> 00:34:01.410
early indication that
fishing is happening

00:34:01.410 --> 00:34:03.320
where it shouldn't,
or if there's

00:34:03.320 --> 00:34:05.810
things they want to focus
on in their larger batch

00:34:05.810 --> 00:34:07.880
analytics that doesn't
look quite right in terms

00:34:07.880 --> 00:34:10.040
of where people
should be fishing

00:34:10.040 --> 00:34:13.480
in a given time of year.

00:34:13.480 --> 00:34:13.980
OK.

00:34:13.980 --> 00:34:20.429
So now, let's look at what
went into that under the hood.

00:34:20.429 --> 00:34:27.400
So here's just kind
of at a high level

00:34:27.400 --> 00:34:32.770
the logic of this second
pipeline that I built. So input

00:34:32.770 --> 00:34:37.540
are these derived features
from the first original Global

00:34:37.540 --> 00:34:38.500
Fishing Watch pipeline.

00:34:38.500 --> 00:34:41.560
And again, using features
in the sense of input

00:34:41.560 --> 00:34:43.900
to their machine learning model.

00:34:43.900 --> 00:34:47.529
And David, remind
me what is MMSI.

00:34:47.529 --> 00:34:48.820
DAVID KROODSMA: Marine Mobile--

00:34:48.820 --> 00:34:49.960
AMY UNRUH: I could never
remember that acronym.

00:34:49.960 --> 00:34:51.520
DAVID KROODSMA:
--Security Number--

00:34:51.520 --> 00:34:52.120
Identifier.

00:34:52.120 --> 00:34:52.859
AMY UNRUH: Yes.

00:34:52.859 --> 00:34:56.560
DAVID KROODSMA: It's given
out by the International

00:34:56.560 --> 00:34:59.702
Telecommunications
Union to boats.

00:34:59.702 --> 00:35:00.910
AMY UNRUH: There you have it.

00:35:00.910 --> 00:35:02.520
There will be a quiz.

00:35:02.520 --> 00:35:07.450
So each such
identification, which

00:35:07.450 --> 00:35:13.910
corresponds to a ship,
a stream of features

00:35:13.910 --> 00:35:16.180
is being generated
for that ship.

00:35:16.180 --> 00:35:19.720
And so this new
Dataflow pipeline--

00:35:19.720 --> 00:35:22.510
it first aggregates
those for each ship

00:35:22.510 --> 00:35:26.010
into a sequence that's
of sufficient length

00:35:26.010 --> 00:35:29.590
to send to the trained
machine learning model.

00:35:29.590 --> 00:35:35.230
And it responds with
indications suggested

00:35:35.230 --> 00:35:38.560
by the blue-colored
cells here of where

00:35:38.560 --> 00:35:45.030
it thinks fishing activity is
happening in that sequence.

00:35:45.030 --> 00:35:49.600
So we know the time where it
thought fishing was happening.

00:35:49.600 --> 00:35:50.710
We know the location.

00:35:53.920 --> 00:35:56.930
And we used the learned
TensorFlow model and Cloud ML

00:35:56.930 --> 00:35:57.680
to do that.

00:35:57.680 --> 00:36:01.610
So then, the next
stage of this Dataflow

00:36:01.610 --> 00:36:10.900
pipeline groups this information
by geographic region.

00:36:10.900 --> 00:36:22.410
So I'm using a representation
called S2 geometry to group the

00:36:22.410 --> 00:36:25.830
predicted fishing activity
into basically, like 1

00:36:25.830 --> 00:36:28.050
kilometer square areas.

00:36:28.050 --> 00:36:32.710
And so by doing that,
we get an indication of,

00:36:32.710 --> 00:36:33.585
what's the magnitude?

00:36:33.585 --> 00:36:35.200
It might be more than one boat.

00:36:35.200 --> 00:36:37.320
What's the magnitude
of fishing in a given

00:36:37.320 --> 00:36:40.120
area at a given point in time?

00:36:40.120 --> 00:36:44.500
And then of course, we want
to analyze this over time.

00:36:44.500 --> 00:36:47.430
And of course, the
magnitude might change over

00:36:47.430 --> 00:36:51.340
time for a given square.

00:36:51.340 --> 00:36:51.840
OK.

00:36:55.930 --> 00:36:58.790
To do this-- now let's
start to look at the nuts

00:36:58.790 --> 00:36:59.820
and bolts a little bit.

00:36:59.820 --> 00:37:03.710
The first thing we did was
composed the two Dataflow

00:37:03.710 --> 00:37:07.685
streaming pipelines,
connecting them by Pub/Sub.

00:37:07.685 --> 00:37:11.150
So the first original feature
extraction pipeline, and then

00:37:11.150 --> 00:37:12.570
this new pipeline.

00:37:12.570 --> 00:37:16.700
To use Cloud ML Engine to do
prediction of fishing activity,

00:37:16.700 --> 00:37:22.880
and then to do, basically,
location-based clustering.

00:37:22.880 --> 00:37:26.930
So this is a really
useful pattern.

00:37:26.930 --> 00:37:30.445
You can kind of think
of Dataflow pipelines

00:37:30.445 --> 00:37:35.720
as like decomposable chunks of
logic, kind of like modules.

00:37:35.720 --> 00:37:38.480
And actually, almost kind
of reminiscent in how

00:37:38.480 --> 00:37:41.780
you hook them together
of Unix pipes as well.

00:37:45.650 --> 00:37:48.110
You can compose them
together, you just

00:37:48.110 --> 00:37:50.810
need to connect their inputs
and outputs essentially.

00:37:50.810 --> 00:37:55.310
And the Dataflow pipelines
can have different owners.

00:37:55.310 --> 00:37:57.740
They can even be in
different projects.

00:37:57.740 --> 00:37:59.600
They can be written in
different languages.

00:37:59.600 --> 00:38:01.520
And that was actually
the case here.

00:38:01.520 --> 00:38:04.690
The original pipeline
was written in Scala

00:38:04.690 --> 00:38:07.150
and the one I built
was written in Java.

00:38:07.150 --> 00:38:12.830
And then, the means of
connection is Pub/Sub.

00:38:12.830 --> 00:38:15.530
So something to
keep in mind then

00:38:15.530 --> 00:38:19.680
is that Apache Beam's unified
model for doing both stream

00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:24.200
and batch processing lets you
use these pipelines that you

00:38:24.200 --> 00:38:25.470
build in different contexts.

00:38:25.470 --> 00:38:28.120
Do you want to hook them
together in streaming mode?

00:38:28.120 --> 00:38:30.080
Do you want to use
them in batch mode?

00:38:30.080 --> 00:38:34.620
You can use essentially
the same pipeline for both.

00:38:34.620 --> 00:38:35.610
OK.

00:38:35.610 --> 00:38:41.600
So another key thing that
we leveraged to do this

00:38:41.600 --> 00:38:45.620
is Apache Beam's
windowing model.

00:38:45.620 --> 00:38:52.180
So if you think of
you've got an unbounded--

00:38:52.180 --> 00:38:54.850
essentially, infinite
stream of data coming in.

00:38:54.850 --> 00:38:59.480
And say it's colored tiles,
kind of like what's suggested up

00:38:59.480 --> 00:39:00.310
top there.

00:39:00.310 --> 00:39:02.890
And say you want to
group them by color.

00:39:02.890 --> 00:39:05.710
You want to group all the green
tiles together and count them.

00:39:05.710 --> 00:39:08.293
You want to group all the yellow
ties together and count them.

00:39:08.293 --> 00:39:14.940
You need to do that in the
context of some defined window,

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:18.304
or else it's ill-defined.

00:39:18.304 --> 00:39:19.470
It's an ill-defined problem.

00:39:19.470 --> 00:39:25.620
So Apache Beam has really
sophisticated and flexible

00:39:25.620 --> 00:39:26.400
windowing model.

00:39:26.400 --> 00:39:29.110
It has different
kinds of windows.

00:39:29.110 --> 00:39:31.980
For the second Dataflow
pipeline I built,

00:39:31.980 --> 00:39:33.750
I used sliding windows.

00:39:33.750 --> 00:39:38.810
And I mentioned this
earlier, it does something

00:39:38.810 --> 00:39:40.000
that's really powerful.

00:39:40.000 --> 00:39:44.960
It lets you say, do you want
a window by data arrival time

00:39:44.960 --> 00:39:48.610
into the pipeline, or
do you want a window

00:39:48.610 --> 00:39:50.570
and do your analysis
with respect

00:39:50.570 --> 00:39:54.360
to when the data was
actually generated?

00:39:54.360 --> 00:39:56.914
And so we're doing
it with respect

00:39:56.914 --> 00:39:58.580
to when the data was
actually generated,

00:39:58.580 --> 00:40:02.270
when the ship actually emitted
that bit of information

00:40:02.270 --> 00:40:05.090
to the satellite.

00:40:05.090 --> 00:40:07.740
Otherwise, we wouldn't get
good results, of course.

00:40:07.740 --> 00:40:08.360
OK.

00:40:08.360 --> 00:40:11.420
And then, the other
key piece then

00:40:11.420 --> 00:40:18.710
is using the Cloud ML
Engine Online Prediction

00:40:18.710 --> 00:40:22.910
Service and the learned
TensorFlow model

00:40:22.910 --> 00:40:26.870
within our pipeline to
actually do these predictions.

00:40:26.870 --> 00:40:32.640
So you can do this with
any TensorFlow model.

00:40:32.640 --> 00:40:37.540
And you just need
to make sure that it

00:40:37.540 --> 00:40:40.144
adheres to a certain convention
when you're building it.

00:40:40.144 --> 00:40:42.310
This is spelled out in the
documentation, obviously.

00:40:42.310 --> 00:40:44.580
When you're building
it, you need

00:40:44.580 --> 00:40:50.670
to add certain input and output
collections to the graph.

00:40:50.670 --> 00:40:52.050
And you can actually
do this kind

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:54.990
of post hoc, which is what I did
with the Global Fishing Watch

00:40:54.990 --> 00:40:55.770
model.

00:40:55.770 --> 00:40:57.510
I took the trained model.

00:40:57.510 --> 00:41:03.570
I essentially loaded it
into a TensorFlow session,

00:41:03.570 --> 00:41:07.744
added these new nodes that
were necessary for Cloud ML,

00:41:07.744 --> 00:41:08.910
and then wrote it out again.

00:41:08.910 --> 00:41:14.200
So you don't need to
do retraining again

00:41:14.200 --> 00:41:17.130
to add these nodes to your
TensorFlow model graph,

00:41:17.130 --> 00:41:19.750
if you've done any
messing with TensorFlow.

00:41:19.750 --> 00:41:22.380
So after you train
your TensorFlow model,

00:41:22.380 --> 00:41:25.350
you have to save it
in a certain way.

00:41:25.350 --> 00:41:29.640
Basically, you need to
export it as a MetaGraph.

00:41:29.640 --> 00:41:31.500
And then once you've
done those two things,

00:41:31.500 --> 00:41:40.260
then Cloud ML Engine knows
how to deploy that model

00:41:40.260 --> 00:41:43.730
and how to use it-- how to
serve it for predictions.

00:41:43.730 --> 00:41:47.570
So that looks like this.

00:41:47.570 --> 00:41:50.300
This gcloud, for those of
you who aren't familiar,

00:41:50.300 --> 00:41:54.410
is the Cloud platform SDK.

00:41:54.410 --> 00:41:57.960
So this is command line.

00:41:57.960 --> 00:41:59.450
You first create your model.

00:41:59.450 --> 00:42:02.330
That's just kind of like a
semantic tag for the model.

00:42:02.330 --> 00:42:08.990
And then you deploy a version,
a named version of that model,

00:42:08.990 --> 00:42:13.984
with a pointer to the path where
you saved the model once you

00:42:13.984 --> 00:42:14.900
were done training it.

00:42:14.900 --> 00:42:20.580
So you did your training.

00:42:20.580 --> 00:42:24.980
Whether it was on Cloud ML
Engine using GPUs as David team

00:42:24.980 --> 00:42:27.270
has been doing or
some other way.

00:42:27.270 --> 00:42:29.540
You've got your
trained, exported model.

00:42:29.540 --> 00:42:35.910
Now you point to that path
and this deploys your model

00:42:35.910 --> 00:42:37.970
to the Cloud.

00:42:37.970 --> 00:42:42.050
So then once you have done that,
and it's successfully deployed,

00:42:42.050 --> 00:42:43.650
you can use it for prediction.

00:42:43.650 --> 00:42:48.440
So you can do that
in all sorts of ways.

00:42:48.440 --> 00:42:52.180
And you can do it just
from the command line,

00:42:52.180 --> 00:42:53.670
gcloud ml predict.

00:42:53.670 --> 00:42:57.620
And just feed it the
model input in the form

00:42:57.620 --> 00:42:59.810
of, say, a JSON file.

00:42:59.810 --> 00:43:04.910
Or you can do what I did
in this Dataflow pipeline,

00:43:04.910 --> 00:43:11.230
is use the Cloud ML rest
API and send it data.

00:43:11.230 --> 00:43:14.780
And call it directly from
the Dataflow pipeline

00:43:14.780 --> 00:43:17.900
and get back the
prediction and use it

00:43:17.900 --> 00:43:20.570
for subsequent processing
in the pipeline.

00:43:20.570 --> 00:43:24.980
So I don't know if
any of you caught

00:43:24.980 --> 00:43:30.080
the keynote this morning,
and the New York taxi--

00:43:30.080 --> 00:43:32.150
very cool New York taxi demo.

00:43:32.150 --> 00:43:35.060
But if you did, you
might have caught that it

00:43:35.060 --> 00:43:36.840
was doing the same thing.

00:43:36.840 --> 00:43:40.280
It was calling the Cloud
ML Engine Online Prediction

00:43:40.280 --> 00:43:45.430
Service from a
Dataflow pipeline.

00:43:45.430 --> 00:43:47.390
OK.

00:43:47.390 --> 00:43:49.680
So just sort of one
more dive a little

00:43:49.680 --> 00:43:54.210
bit further to look at
what's the UI for this.

00:43:54.210 --> 00:43:56.220
If you were going to
build this, what would you

00:43:56.220 --> 00:43:57.638
see in the Cloud console?

00:44:07.260 --> 00:44:10.950
Here is that pipeline
happily running.

00:44:10.950 --> 00:44:16.160
And you can see
the call ml engine

00:44:16.160 --> 00:44:18.500
part is where we're
actually making

00:44:18.500 --> 00:44:25.720
the rest API calls out to the
Cloud ML Prediction Service.

00:44:25.720 --> 00:44:28.582
And then, we're taking
that information.

00:44:28.582 --> 00:44:31.040
Basically, where do we think
fishing activity is happening?

00:44:31.040 --> 00:44:35.050
And the rest of the
pipeline is grouping it

00:44:35.050 --> 00:44:39.960
according to these
S2 geometry regions,

00:44:39.960 --> 00:44:44.810
and then doing a streaming
right to BigQuery

00:44:44.810 --> 00:44:47.840
to record what we have inferred.

00:44:47.840 --> 00:44:57.000
So here is a dashboard
for the Cloud ML Online

00:44:57.000 --> 00:44:58.240
Prediction Request.

00:44:58.240 --> 00:45:01.410
So you can see, I'm kind
of like cutting it close.

00:45:01.410 --> 00:45:04.980
I keep coming close to my
quota, but I'm carefully

00:45:04.980 --> 00:45:07.590
backing off when I do.

00:45:07.590 --> 00:45:10.200
So this just lets
you track what's

00:45:10.200 --> 00:45:14.750
going on with your
deployed TensorFlow models.

00:45:14.750 --> 00:45:19.750
And then, this last
tab here is BigQuery,

00:45:19.750 --> 00:45:23.950
where all of this derived
information is getting written.

00:45:23.950 --> 00:45:25.760
BigQuery has a streaming API.

00:45:25.760 --> 00:45:29.000
And then we can use it
for whatever we like,

00:45:29.000 --> 00:45:32.570
including building a map
like the one I showed you.

00:45:32.570 --> 00:45:37.180
So I'm going to try to run this
