00:46:50.890 --> 00:46:54.880
which is using the deployed
model through the Cloud ML API

00:46:54.880 --> 00:46:58.701
to get those predictions.

00:46:58.701 --> 00:46:59.200
OK.

00:46:59.200 --> 00:47:05.480
So just to summarize what I
did with the new streaming

00:47:05.480 --> 00:47:08.120
pipeline, there were
a few cool things

00:47:08.120 --> 00:47:10.170
that made it all fit together.

00:47:10.170 --> 00:47:16.874
So the first thing was Beam's
unified batch streaming model.

00:47:16.874 --> 00:47:18.290
There wasn't really
very much that

00:47:18.290 --> 00:47:22.130
had to be done before I started
building on top of this.

00:47:22.130 --> 00:47:23.330
So that was one thing.

00:47:23.330 --> 00:47:26.240
The ability to easily
compose pipeline's,

00:47:26.240 --> 00:47:30.130
kind of glue them
together, using Pub/Sub.

00:47:30.130 --> 00:47:37.000
Beam's ability to support
reasoning about event time

00:47:37.000 --> 00:47:39.670
in terms of when the
data was actually

00:47:39.670 --> 00:47:42.340
generated, not when it arrived.

00:47:42.340 --> 00:47:46.570
And then of course, Cloud
ML Engine Online Prediction

00:47:46.570 --> 00:47:47.650
Service.

00:47:47.650 --> 00:47:54.310
To use our trained model in this
new pipeline and get results

00:47:54.310 --> 00:47:57.550
and just incorporate
it into the analysis.

00:47:57.550 --> 00:47:58.060
OK.

00:47:58.060 --> 00:48:03.340
